      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1018",
      "html": "<ul><li><strong>Scientific name:</strong> Megalancistrus barrae (Steindachner, 1910)</li><li><strong>Common names:</strong> Black Spiny Ashiura Pleco, Azzura Pleco, Giant Ancistrus</li><li><strong>Type locality:</strong> Rio São Francisco near Barra, Brazil.</li><li><strong>Synonym:</strong> Ancistrus barrae</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> 385 mm (15.2\") SL</li><li><strong>Etymology:</strong> Greek, megas, megalos = great + Greek, agkistron = hook, in this case meaning \"big Ancistrus\". Named for the Rio São Francisco near Barra in Brazil, the type locality.</li><li><strong>IUCN Red List:</strong> Least Concern (last assessed 2018).</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1018",
      "html": "<p>South America: São Francisco River basin, Brazil.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1018",
      "html": "<p>Breeding: unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1018",
      "html": "<p>A colossal pleco worthy of the generic name. Can be differentiated from other large loricariids by the presence of evertible interopercular odontodes.</p><p>This species' background colour is dark brown to black; those more commonly exported are black, and often they are exported under the nomen nudem \"Megalancistrus etentaculatus\".</p>"
    },
